WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:06.890
In the early 1990s, director James Cameron
convinced Carolco Pictures — which produced

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.590
movies such as Terminator 2, Total Recall,
and Rambo — to option the film rights to

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:16.330
Spider-Man. But Cameron's production was ultimately
tangled in an inescapable web. Why couldn't

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.089
one of Hollywood's most powerful directors
get this film made?

00:00:20.089 --> 00:00:25.890
"Oh no, no, ah!"

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:26.630
Too edgy

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:31.159
Cameron's plans for Spider-Man have become
a Hollywood legend. According to Rebecca Keegan's

00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:35.720
book The Futurist: The Life and Films of James
Cameron, Spidey creator Stan Lee absolutely

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.120
loved Cameron's treatment. Ultimately, many
of Cameron's own ideas—like Spidey's organic

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:44.610
web-shooters and Uncle Ben's carjacking death—made
it into the Sam Raimi-directed Spider-Man

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.360
movie from 2002.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.780
But for every one of Cameron's good ideas,
there were at least three that were...not.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.620
Cameron called for Peter to curse like a sailor,
which would've resulted in an R-rating. And

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.040
then there was a scene where Peter peeped
in Mary Jane's window as she dressed, and

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.290
one where Spidey and MJ get...intimate on
the Brooklyn Bridge while Peter describes

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.450
the creepy ways spiders mate.

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.290
Uh, what?

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.760
"Aaaahhh!"

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.460
Low budget

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.570
Cameron would've likely made Spider-Man right
after Terminator 2, which had a $100 million

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:22.710
budget. According to Janet Wasko's How Hollywood
Works, Carolco only committed to a $50 million

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.880
budget, a huge step backwards for him. And
if the reported budgets of his next two films

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.560
- $100 million for True Lies and a record-setting
$200 million for Titanic - tell us anything,

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.720
it's that Spidey's balance sheet wasn't going
to fly.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.830
Bad casting

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:40.979
Rumor has it that Cameron wanted to cast Arnold
Schwarzenegger as Doctor Octopus. Schwarzenegger

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:45.390
even hinted at his involvement in an interview
with Empire, only going so far as to vaguely

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.880
say the the studio "went in another direction."

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.770
There are yet more rumors and reports of Cameron's
dream cast for the film...and one of them

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:56.320
is a pretty strange choice. Edward Furlong,
whose big break came as John Connor in Cameron's

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.890
Terminator 2, was reportedly the director's
pick for Peter Parker, and Leonardo DiCaprio

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:05.549
was to play Harry Osborne. Of course, Leo
wouldn't play second fiddle to just anyone.

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:08.670
Johnny Depp? Sure. Eddie Furlong? Not a chance.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:12.599
The report also pegs Drew Barrymore as Gwen
Stacey and J. Jonah Jameson would've been

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:15.360
played by R. Lee Ermey—the drill sergeant
in Full Metal Jacket.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.670
"You will not laugh! You will not cry! You
will learn by the numbers! I will teach you!"

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.440
That would have been perfect, but it wouldn't
have been enough to make up for tanking the

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.340
principal cast.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:25.980
Litigation

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.720
While the script, casting, and budget issues
all could've become later stumbling blocks,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:35.150
what ultimately squashed Cameron's Spider-Man
were legal issues. Cameron told Collider that

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:38.910
when Carolco collapsed a few years after they
acquired the rights to Spider-Man, he already

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:43.590
had Titanic in his sights. Moreover, he no
longer wanted to pursue Spider-Man because

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:48.250
of all the competing interests for the rights
amid Carolco's bankruptcy...and Marvel's.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.140
Apparently a number of former writers and
producers all claimed to have had a hand in

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.830
developing the project, and they all came
calling.

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:59.280
Marvel's own 1996 bankruptcy process scattered
multiple holding deals to the wind. According

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.800
to How Hollywood Works, after Marvel recovered
from bankruptcy, they basically paid off everyone

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:07.080
who was trying to sue for the rights to Spider-Man,
which freed them up to sell the rights to

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:07.819
Sony.

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:11.700
Meanwhile, Cameron turned one of the greatest
human tragedies of all time into a billion-dollar

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.250
smash, so he did just fine.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:17.410
"I'm the king of the world! Woooo! Woo! Woo!"

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:22.140
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.670
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know who should've starred in James Cameron's

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:26.500
Spider-Man movie...

